        "viewMode": "Single profile",
        "profileType": "Slow endergonic (d)",
        "barrierHeight": 0.95,
        "productEnergy": 0.5
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "ΔG‡ (kinetics) and ΔG° (thermodynamics) are independent: any barrier height can pair with any product energy, which is why 'fast' and 'favorable' are separate questions about a reaction.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Default view is a 2×2 grid of four equal panels labeled (a) top-left, (b) top-right, (c) bottom-left, (d) bottom-right, exactly reproducing the textbook layout; 'Single profile' view replaces the grid with one enlarged panel (same styling) plus the sliders. Every panel: pale blue field, cream-to-orange vertical 'Energy' arrow at left, and a horizontal axis below whose magenta 'Reaction progress' text must sit clear of the magenta arrowhead — put the arrowhead entirely to the right of the text or directly beneath it, never overlapping the glyphs. No numeric ticks anywhere. The magenta curve is a smooth single-humped profile with flat reactant and product plateaus. Dashed reference lines are SHORT local segments only: a brief segment near the reactant plateau at the left, one near the peak, one near the product plateau at the right — each just long enough to anchor its arrow, never spanning most of the field width. The ΔG‡ double-arrow spans reactant to peak segment near the rising slope; the ΔG° double-arrow spans reactant to product segment positioned near the MIDDLE of the curve (roughly above the hump's descending region), matching source panels a/b — do not push it to the far right edge. In single view, selecting a profile type snaps the sliders to that panel's canonical values and shows the matching (a)–(d) label; dragging either slider redraws the curve smoothly and repositions the dashed segments and arrows live, keeping the peak clearly above both endpoints at all settings."
}

